# Third-party notices

## v-analyzer (MIT)

`engine/` and `tree_sitter_v/` are ported from v-analyzer, the V language server
by the V Open Source Community Association, at commit 925d457.

The license text is in `LICENSES/v-analyzer-MIT/LICENSE`. Every ported file
carries a two-line header naming v-analyzer, that commit, and the license.

The port is not a copy. The modules move out of the `src/` tree v-analyzer used,
which V 0.5.2 no longer builds, and the code is adapted where the language
moved.

`tree_sitter_v/` is vendored whole, including the tree-sitter runtime and the V
grammar in C, because the engine's parser imports `tree_sitter_v.bindings` and
that package pulls its own C through `#flag` lines. Nothing in it comes from the
system.

## The V compiler

The compiler is the one piece visor runs rather than carries. It has to be a
master build, because no published release carries `vlib/json2` and `lsp/`
imports it. In a directory you do not mind keeping around:

```sh
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/vlang/v
cd v
make
```

That leaves `./v` in the clone. Put that directory on `PATH` ahead of any
release build. The version line will not tell you which one you got: a master
build answers `V 0.5.2` as well, differing only in the commit hash that follows
it. What separates them is `vlib/json2` sitting next to the compiler, and CI
fails on exactly that. `ROADMAP.md` says why the floor sits there and when 0.1.x
lowers it.
